---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";

const snapshot = [
  { label: "Client / Program", value: "Merrill Lynch / Bank of America" },
  { label: "Role", value: "Sr. UX/UI Consultant" },
  { label: "Product", value: "Internal Product Tools / Design System" },
  { label: "Industry", value: "Financial Services / Enterprise" },
  { label: "Scale", value: "6-person cross-functional team" },
];

const methodology = [
  "Frame - Align and define",
  "Discover - Research and empathize",
  "Structure - Information architecture",
  "Ideate - Explore concepts",
  "Interact - Optimize flows",
  "Design - Visual and systems",
  "Validate - Test and reduce risk",
  "Launch - Build and QA",
  "Improve - Measure and learn",
];

const deliverables = [
  "Cross-platform enterprise design system",
  "Comprehensive pattern library",
  "Production-ready component codebase",
  "Design and development documentation",
  "FactSet data integration workflow considerations",
  "Governance standards and guidelines",
];

const methods = [
  "Sketch",
  "Figma",
  "Pattern libraries",
  "Component documentation",
  "Design system governance",
  "Usability testing",
  "Enterprise workflow analysis",
];

const outcomes = [
  "Reduced design and development duplication through shared patterns",
  "Increased consistency across advisor-facing tools",
  "Supported lower long-term maintenance overhead through reusable components",
  "Improved financial advisor workflow consistency",
  "Supported faster delivery of new features through shared design and development foundations",
  "Enhanced scalability for future internal products",
];

const workedWell = [
  "Establishing a comprehensive pattern library created a stronger foundation for consistency.",
  "Collaboration between design and development helped translate design patterns into reusable product foundations.",
  "A shared design system improved alignment across teams working on advisor-facing tools.",
];

const improvements = [
  "Faster feedback loops during testing would have helped refine components more efficiently.",
  "Greater upfront stakeholder buy-in could have accelerated adoption of the design system.",
];

const takeaways = [
  "User feedback is essential when shaping design systems for complex enterprise tools.",
  "Design systems need governance, not just components.",
  "Cross-disciplinary collaboration is critical when turning design standards into usable product infrastructure.",
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="Enterprise Design System | Jorge Obando"
  description="Merrill Lynch and Bank of America case study by Jorge Obando, covering enterprise design systems, advisor-facing tools, pattern libraries, governance, and design-engineering collaboration."
  ogImage="/images/merrill-lynch-enterprise-design-system-case-study.png"
>
  <section class="case-study-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Merrill Lynch / Bank of America</p>
        <h1>Enterprise Design System</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          An enterprise design system for internal financial advisor tools, created to
          reduce fragmented experiences, improve consistency, and support more
          scalable product delivery across advisor-facing platforms.
        </p>
      </div>

      <figure class="hero-image">
        <img
          src="/images/merrill-lynch-enterprise-design-system-case-study.png"
          alt="Merrill Lynch enterprise advisor platform interface screenshot"
        />
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="snapshot-grid" aria-label="Case study snapshot">
      {snapshot.map((item) => (
        <article class="snapshot-card">
          <p class="snapshot-label">{item.label}</p>
          <p class="snapshot-value">{item.value}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        Merrill Lynch faced a challenge with internal financial advisor tools that had
        been developed across different verticals and product teams. This created
        inconsistent design patterns, redundant components, fragmented experiences,
        and duplicated design and development effort.
      </p>
      <p>
        As Sr. UX/UI Consultant, Jorge supported the development of an enterprise
        design system intended to create a more cohesive framework for advisor-facing
        tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        Merrill Lynch, a division of Bank of America, needed to improve the
        consistency and scalability of internal enterprise tools used by financial
        advisors. The objective was to create a cohesive enterprise design system that
        could reduce fragmented user experiences, support reusable components, improve
        collaboration between design and engineering, and establish governance for
        future product work.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The real problem</h2>
      <p>
        Merrill Lynch, a division of Bank of America, had a set of internal enterprise
        financial advisor tools that had evolved in silos across different verticals.
      </p>
      <p>
        The result was inconsistent design patterns, redundant components, fragmented
        user experiences, and duplicated effort across teams.
      </p>
      <p>
        For financial advisors, the lack of a standardized framework created friction
        when moving across tools and workflows. For the organization, it created
        maintenance overhead and made it harder to scale product improvements
        consistently.
      </p>
      <p>
        The core challenge was not simply visual consistency. It was creating a
        shared design and delivery foundation that could support advisor productivity,
        product scalability, and long-term governance.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Role and leadership</h2>
      <p>
        As Sr. UX/UI Consultant, Jorge helped shape the design system strategy and
        worked with cross-functional stakeholders across design, product, and
        engineering.
      </p>
      <p>
        His responsibilities included contributing to design principles, component
        patterns, documentation standards, governance guidance, and alignment between
        design and development teams.
      </p>
      <p>
        The role required translating fragmented advisor tool experiences into
        reusable patterns and helping establish a shared framework that could support
        future product delivery.
      </p>
      <p>
        The work involved a 6-person cross-functional team. Collaboration was
        colocated, supporting close coordination between design, product, and
        engineering stakeholders throughout design, prototyping, testing,
        documentation, and implementation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Methodology</h2>
      <p>
        The work followed a research-led, structure-first design process with
        continuous validation. The approach emphasized research over opinion,
        structure before surface, and continuous usability validation.
      </p>
      <div class="cards-grid three-up">
        {methodology.map((item) => (
          <article class="info-card compact-card">
            <p>{item}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Key deliverables</h2>
      <div class="outcomes-card">
        <ul>
          {deliverables.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Design process</h2>
      <div class="cards-grid one-up">
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Research and discovery</h3>
          <p>
            The research approach included user interviews, usability testing with
            financial advisors, and a detailed audit of existing tools.
          </p>
          <p>
            The team cataloged design inconsistencies, redundant components, and
            workflow fragmentation across internal advisor platforms.
          </p>
          <p>
            Key insights showed that advisors had to navigate disparate systems and
            inconsistent interaction patterns, creating unnecessary friction in their
            daily work.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Information architecture</h3>
          <p>
            The information architecture strategy focused on creating a clear
            organizational structure for the design system.
          </p>
          <p>
            The team established taxonomy for components, patterns, and documentation
            so that designers and developers could find, understand, and reuse assets
            more easily.
          </p>
          <p>
            A centralized pattern library served as a shared source of truth, helping
            reduce confusion across teams and improve consistency across product
            verticals.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Prototyping and testing</h3>
          <p>
            The team used iterative prototyping methods to explore key components and
            advisor workflows.
          </p>
          <p>
            High-fidelity prototypes were created for critical patterns and reviewed
            with stakeholders and users.
          </p>
          <p>
            Testing helped validate design assumptions, identify usability issues, and
            refine components before broader implementation.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Iterations and refinements</h3>
          <p>
            The project went through multiple iterations based on user feedback and
            stakeholder review.
          </p>
          <p>
            Initial patterns and workflow designs were refined to improve clarity,
            usability, and guidance.
          </p>
          <p>
            Feedback from advisors highlighted areas where contextual help, clearer
            interaction states, and better pattern documentation could improve the
            overall experience.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Technologies and methods</h2>
      <div class="tag-list" aria-label="Technologies and methods">
        {methods.map((item) => (
          <span>{item}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Outcomes</h2>
      <p>
        The design system work supported Merrill Lynch's goals around reducing design
        and development duplication, improving operational consistency, and creating a
        more scalable foundation for advisor-facing tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        By establishing shared patterns, documentation, and governance, the initiative
        helped reduce fragmentation across tools and supported more consistent product
        delivery across verticals.
      </p>
      <div class="outcomes-card">
        <ul>
          {outcomes.map((item) => (
            <li>{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Reflection</h2>
      <div class="cards-grid one-up">
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>What worked well</h3>
          <ul>
            {workedWell.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Areas for improvement</h3>
          <ul>
            {improvements.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="info-card">
          <h3>Key takeaways</h3>
          <ul>
            {takeaways.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Why this matters</h2>
      <p>
        Enterprise design systems are not only about visual consistency. They create
        shared foundations for governance, reuse, collaboration, and scalable delivery
        across complex product organizations.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Related Thinking</h2>
      <section class="article-links" aria-label="Related thinking and next navigation">
        <a href="/thinking/governed-product-system">
          Designing governed product systems for scale and alignment
        </a>
        <a href="/thinking/measuring-what-matters">
          Why measuring outcomes - not outputs - changes execution
        </a>
        <a href="/case-studies">
          Return to all case studies
        </a>
      </section>
    </section>
  </section>
</MainLayout>

<style>
  .case-study-page {
    padding: 2.5rem 0 5rem;
  }

  .hero {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(320px, 0.9fr);
    gap: 2rem;
    align-items: end;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .hero-copy {
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #a33a3a;
  }

  .hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 4.15rem);
    line-height: 1.05;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  }

  .lead {
    margin-top: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: #555;
    max-width: 720px;
  }

  .hero-image {
    margin: 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--divider);
    border-radius: 18px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .hero-image img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    min-height: 280px;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .snapshot-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .snapshot-card,
  .info-card,
  .outcomes-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--divider);
    border-radius: 16px;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .snapshot-card {
    padding: 1.2rem;
  }

  .snapshot-label {
    margin: 0 0 0.45rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #777;
  }

  .snapshot-value {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #222;
  }

  .content-section {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--divider);
  }

  .content-section h2 {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 2.2vw, 2.4rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .content-section p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    color: #444;
    line-height: 1.8;
    max-width: 820px;
  }

  .cards-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  .cards-grid.three-up {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  .cards-grid.one-up {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  .info-card {
    padding: 1.4rem;
  }

  .compact-card {
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
  }

  .info-card h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.65rem;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }

  .info-card p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    max-width: none;
  }

  .info-card p:last-child,
  .compact-card p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .info-card ul,
  .outcomes-card ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
  }

  .info-card li,
  .outcomes-card li {
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: #444;
  }

  .info-card li:last-child,
  .outcomes-card li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .outcomes-card {
    padding: 1.35rem 1.4rem;
  }

  .tag-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    max-width: 820px;
  }

  .tag-list span {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--divider);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.75rem;
    color: #444;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.3;
    background: #fff;
  }

  .article-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem 1.5rem;
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--divider);
    max-width: 760px;
  }

  .article-links a {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #111;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 960px) {
    .hero {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      align-items: start;
    }

    .snapshot-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .cards-grid.three-up {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .case-study-page {
      padding: 1.5rem 0 4rem;
    }

    .snapshot-grid,
    .cards-grid.three-up {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .lead {
      font-size: 1.02rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
    }

    .hero-image img {
      min-height: 220px;
    }

    .article-links {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.35rem;
    }
  }
</style>
